---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ShellRecipe from '../components/ShellRecipe.astro';
---

<Base title="ExCSV CLI Cookbook" description="Copy-paste grep/awk/cut one-liners for reading ExCSV files — schema, stats, SQL, ZIP previews, and pack columns — with no library at all.">
  <h1>CLI Cookbook</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    An ExCSV file is still plain, delimiter-separated text — every metadata line starts with
    <code>#</code>, so <code>grep</code>, <code>awk</code>, and <code>cut</code> already know how
    to skim it. You don't need a parser to answer "how many rows," "what's column 3 called," or
    "what's the total" — one shell command does it. Reach for a real library
    (<a href="/tools/">Go</a> or <a href="/tools/">Python</a>) once you need error handling,
    validation, or to write files back out; these recipes are for the terminal, a notebook cell,
    or a shell script glued into a bigger pipeline.
  </p>
  <p class="intro">
    Every command below is verified against the format's own <a href="https://github.com/boligolov/excsv/tree/master/fixtures" target="_blank" rel="noopener">fixture corpus</a>.
    Save the sample below as <code>orders.excsv</code> and follow along.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock title="orders.excsv" description="Inline ExCSV — the sample every recipe in the first sections runs against.">
{`#!excsv version=0.5 rows=2 schema=excsv sql-dialect=mysql
#@author: author@example.com
#@source: sales_db.orders
#@created: 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z
#@exported: 2026-03-24T12:00:00Z
#@license: CC-BY-4.0
#@tool: excsv-cli/0.2.0
#@tags: sales,Q1
#$ddl: CREATE TABLE orders (id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT, customer VARCHAR(100), amount DECIMAL(8,2)) ENGINE=InnoDB
#$ddl-mysql: CREATE TABLE orders (id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT, amount DECIMAL(8,2)) ENGINE=InnoDB
#$ddl-postgres: CREATE TABLE orders (id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, amount NUMERIC(8,2))
#$ddl-clickhouse: CREATE TABLE orders (id UInt64, amount Decimal(8,2)) ENGINE = MergeTree() ORDER BY id
#$dql: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE amount > 0
#column name=id type=int unique=1
#column name=customer type=string required=1 len_max=100
#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD min=0
#%count_nonnull: 2,2,2
#%count_null: 0,0,0
#%count_distinct: 2,2,2
#%sum: ,,120.50
#%avg: ,,60.25
#%min: ,,20.50
#%max: ,,100.00
id,customer,amount
1,Acme,100.00
2,Globex,20.50`}
  </CodeBlock>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Read the data</h2>
    <p class="section-note">The <code>#</code> lines are comments. Drop them and it's a CSV file, full stop.</p>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Strip the metadata — get plain CSV"
      command={`grep -v '^#' orders.excsv`}
      output={`id,customer,amount
1,Acme,100.00
2,Globex,20.50`}
    >
      Every ExCSV reader already does this on your behalf. If the file has a UTF-8 BOM, see the <a href="#bom">BOM gotcha</a> below first.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Pretty-print as an aligned table"
      command={`grep -v '^#' orders.excsv | column -t -s,`}
      output={`id  customer  amount
1   Acme      100.00
2   Globex    20.50`}
    />

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Pull one column by name"
      command={`grep -v '^#' orders.excsv | awk -F, -v col=amount 'NR==1{for(i=1;i<=NF;i++) if($i==col) c=i} {print $c}'`}
      output={`amount
100.00
20.50`}
    >
      Looks up the position from the header row instead of hard-coding a column index, so it survives reordered columns.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Filter rows by a numeric threshold, by column name"
      command={`grep -v '^#' orders.excsv | awk -F, -v col=amount -v min=50 'NR==1{for(i=1;i<=NF;i++) if($i==col) c=i; print; next} $c+0>min'`}
      output={`id,customer,amount
1,Acme,100.00`}
    />
  </section>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Read the header</h2>
    <p class="section-note">The first line carries everything about the file as a whole — version, delimiter, row count, checksum. It's one long line; here's how to make it skimmable.</p>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Print the header, one attribute per line"
      command={`head -1 orders.excsv | tr ' ' '\\n'`}
      output={`#!excsv
version=0.5
rows=2
schema=excsv
sql-dialect=mysql`}
    >
      Turns a long <code>key=value key=value …</code> line into a scannable list. (A quoted value with a space inside — rare — would get split too; check for <code>"</code> before relying on this in a script.)
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Row count"
      command={`grep -v '^#' orders.excsv | tail -n +2 | wc -l`}
      output={`2`}
    />
  </section>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Trust the stats — or check them yourself</h2>
    <p class="section-note">Sums, averages, and null counts ride along in the header, lined up with the columns in the same order. Find a column's position once, then reuse it.</p>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Find a column's position"
      command={`grep '^#column' orders.excsv | nl -ba -s': '`}
      output={`     1: #column name=id type=int unique=1
     2: #column name=customer type=string required=1 len_max=100
     3: #column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD min=0`}
    >
      <code>amount</code> is the 3rd <code>#column</code> line.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Read a stat at that position"
      command={`grep '^#%sum:' orders.excsv | cut -d' ' -f2 | cut -d, -f3`}
      output={`120.50`}
    >
      The aggregation lines list one value per column, in the same order as the <code>#column</code> lines above — position 3 is <code>amount</code> in both. Swap <code>#%sum:</code> for <code>#%avg:</code>, <code>#%min:</code>, <code>#%max:</code>, <code>#%count_null:</code>, … — same trick, same position.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Null count for a different column, same trick"
      command={`grep '^#%count_null:' orders.excsv | cut -d' ' -f2 | cut -d, -f2`}
      output={`0`}
    >
      <code>customer</code> is position 2, so this cuts field 2 instead of field 3.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Recompute a total yourself, as a sanity check"
      command={`grep -v '^#' orders.excsv | awk -F, -v col=amount 'NR==1{for(i=1;i<=NF;i++) if($i==col) c=i; next} {s+=$c} END{printf "%.2f\\n", s}'`}
      output={`120.50`}
    >
      If this disagrees with the stored <code>#%sum:</code>, the file's stats are stale — the data changed after the aggregates were written.
    </ShellRecipe>
  </section>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Schema, provenance, and meaning</h2>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="List column names"
      command={`grep '^#column' orders.excsv | sed -E 's/.*name=([^ ]+).*/\\1/'`}
      output={`id
customer
amount`}
    />

    <ShellRecipe
      title="List name:type pairs"
      command={`grep '^#column' orders.excsv | sed -E 's/.*name=([^ ]+) type=([^ ]+).*/\\1:\\2/'`}
      output={`id:int
customer:string
amount:decimal`}
    />

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Who exported this, and when?"
      command={`grep '^#@' orders.excsv`}
      output={`#@author: author@example.com
#@source: sales_db.orders
#@created: 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z
#@exported: 2026-03-24T12:00:00Z
#@license: CC-BY-4.0
#@tool: excsv-cli/0.2.0
#@tags: sales,Q1`}
    />
  </section>

  <CodeBlock title="sales.excsv" description="A second sample — adds #@grain and per-column role= / agg= hints for the next two recipes.">
{`#!excsv version=0.5 header=1 rows=4
#@source: sales_db.orders
#@grain: one row per order
#column name=order_id type=long unique=1 role=id
#column name=status type=string role=dimension enum=pending|completed|cancelled
#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD role=measure agg=sum
#column name=balance type=decimal unit=USD role=measure agg=avg
#column name=created_at type=datetime role=time
#%count_nonnull: 4,4,4,4,4
#%sum: ,,1050.50,,
order_id,status,amount,balance,created_at
1,completed,500.00,1200.00,2026-01-15T09:30:00Z
2,completed,300.50,900.00,2026-02-20T14:00:00Z
3,pending,200.00,700.00,2026-03-01T11:45:00Z
4,cancelled,50.00,650.00,2026-03-10T16:20:00Z`}
  </CodeBlock>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Semantics: grain, roles, aggregation</h2>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="What does one row mean?"
      command={`grep '^#@grain:' sales.excsv | sed 's/^#@grain: //'`}
      output={`one row per order`}
    />

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Which columns are safe to sum?"
      command={`grep '^#column' sales.excsv | grep 'agg=sum' | sed -E 's/.*name=([^ ]+).*/\\1/'`}
      output={`amount`}
    >
      <code>balance</code> is a <code>measure</code> too, but <code>agg=avg</code> — summing it across rows would double-count. <code>order_id</code> has <code>role=id</code>: never aggregate it at all. This is the difference between a plain CSV and one that tells an LLM (or a teammate) what not to do with it.
    </ShellRecipe>
  </section>

  <CodeBlock title="notes.excsv" description="Human comment lines (##) mixed in with machine metadata.">
{`#!excsv version=0.5 rows=1
## this is a human note
#@source: demo
## comment between meta kinds
#column name=id type=int
#column name=name type=string
## another human note
id,name
1,Alice`}
  </CodeBlock>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Human notes, and a real-world gotcha</h2>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Read the notes a person left behind"
      command={`grep '^##' notes.excsv | sed 's/^## //'`}
      output={`this is a human note
comment between meta kinds
another human note`}
    >
      <code>##</code> lines are for people, not tooling — they can appear anywhere between the machine metadata lines.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <div id="bom">
      <ShellRecipe
        title="The BOM gotcha"
        command={`printf '\\xef\\xbb\\xbf#!excsv version=0.5 rows=1\\n#column name=id type=int\\nid\\n1\\n' > bom.excsv
grep -v '^#' bom.excsv`}
        output={`#!excsv version=0.5 rows=1
id
1`}
      >
        Plenty of real-world CSV exports (Excel especially) carry a UTF-8 byte-order mark, and it sits <em>before</em> the <code>#</code> on line 1 — so <code>grep -v '^#'</code> doesn't match line 1 at all, and the un-stripped header leaks into the "clean" output above (it's just invisible: the BOM is a zero-width character). Fix it with <code>sed '1s/^\xef\xbb\xbf//'</code> before piping into <code>grep -v '^#'</code>.
      </ShellRecipe>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>SQL companions</h2>
    <p class="section-note">The DDL that created the table travels with the data. No separate migration file to lose track of.</p>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Recreate the table"
      command={`grep '^#\\$ddl:' orders.excsv | sed 's/^#\\$ddl: //' | psql mydb`}
    >
      Pulls the unqualified <code>#$ddl:</code> line — the portable fallback — and pipes it straight into <code>psql</code> (swap for <code>mysql</code>, <code>clickhouse-client</code>, …). See <a href="/spec/#sql">SQL companions</a>.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="DDL for a specific dialect, with fallback"
      command={`grep -E '^#\\$ddl:|^#\\$ddl-postgres:' orders.excsv | sed -E 's/^#\\$ddl(-postgres)?: //'`}
      output={`CREATE TABLE orders (id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT, customer VARCHAR(100), amount DECIMAL(8,2)) ENGINE=InnoDB
CREATE TABLE orders (id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, amount NUMERIC(8,2))`}
    >
      A consumer targeting <code>postgres</code> runs the unqualified line, then the <code>-postgres</code> override — same rule the spec defines for any dialect.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Which dialects does this file cover?"
      command={`grep -o '^#\\$ddl-[a-z0-9-]*' orders.excsv | sed 's/^#\\$ddl-//'`}
      output={`mysql
postgres
clickhouse`}
    />
  </section>

  <CodeBlock title="report.excsv (sidecar) + report.csv" description="The sidecar profile: metadata in one file, the untouched original in another.">
{`# report.excsv
#!excsv version=0.5 delim=comma header=1 rows=2 reference=report.csv
#@source: fixtures_db.orders
#column name=id type=int
#column name=customer type=string
#column name=amount type=decimal
#%sum: ,,750.50

# report.csv — byte-for-byte original, never touched
id,customer,amount
1,Acme,500.00
2,Globex,250.50`}
  </CodeBlock>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Sidecar — annotate a file you can't touch</h2>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Find the file this sidecar describes"
      command={`grep -o 'reference=[^ ]*' report.excsv`}
      output={`reference=report.csv`}
    />

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Tell inline from sidecar"
      command={`grep -q 'reference=' report.excsv && echo sidecar || echo inline`}
      output={`sidecar`}
    >
      An inline file (like <code>orders.excsv</code> at the top of this page) has no <code>reference=</code> key — it carries its own rows.
    </ShellRecipe>
  </section>

  <CodeBlock title="signed.excsv" description="A minimal file carrying an integrity checksum.">
{`#!excsv version=0.5 checksum=sha256:7cde7fb64fd82bd152710cf238e017b9ab46c0592483edc067ba4f6c75fac108 rows=1
id
1`}
  </CodeBlock>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Checksum</h2>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Extract the declared checksum"
      command={`grep -o 'checksum=[^ ]*' signed.excsv`}
      output={`checksum=sha256:7cde7fb64fd82bd152710cf238e017b9ab46c0592483edc067ba4f6c75fac108`}
    >
      Presence alone tells you the file claims integrity protection; verifying it means recomputing the hash over the canonical form — see <a href="/spec/#header">the header reference</a> or just use a library.
    </ShellRecipe>
  </section>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>ZIP — preview a compressed file without unzipping it</h2>
    <p class="section-note">
      A <code>.excsv.zip</code> mirrors its header, columns, and stats into the archive's <strong>comment field</strong>.
      Browsing a folder of zipped exports, you see what each one holds without extracting a single byte.
      Zip the <code>orders.excsv</code> from the top of this page as <code>orders.excsv.zip</code> and try:
    </p>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Peek the schema and stats"
      command={`zipinfo -z orders.excsv.zip`}
      output={`Archive:  orders.excsv.zip
#!excsv version=0.5 rows=2 schema=excsv sql-dialect=mysql
#@author: author@example.com
#@source: sales_db.orders
#@created: 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z
#@exported: 2026-03-24T12:00:00Z
#@license: CC-BY-4.0
#@tool: excsv-cli/0.2.0
#@tags: sales,Q1
#$ddl: CREATE TABLE orders (id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT, customer VARCHAR(100), amount DECIMAL(8,2)) ENGINE=InnoDB
#$ddl-mysql: CREATE TABLE orders (id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT, amount DECIMAL(8,2)) ENGINE=InnoDB
#$ddl-postgres: CREATE TABLE orders (id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, amount NUMERIC(8,2))
#$ddl-clickhouse: CREATE TABLE orders (id UInt64, amount Decimal(8,2)) ENGINE = MergeTree() ORDER BY id
#$dql: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE amount > 0
#column name=id type=int unique=1
#column name=customer type=string required=1 len_max=100
#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD min=0
#%count_nonnull: 2,2,2
#%count_null: 0,0,0
#%count_distinct: 2,2,2
#%sum: ,,120.50
#%avg: ,,60.25
#%min: ,,20.50
#%max: ,,100.00
Zip file size: 1627 bytes, number of entries: 1
...`}
    >
      <code>unzip -z</code> works the same way. Only the header, columns, SQL, and aggregates are mirrored — never the data rows.
    </ShellRecipe>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Pull clean CSV straight out of the zip"
      command={`unzip -p orders.excsv.zip orders.excsv | grep -v '^#'`}
      output={`id,customer,amount
1,Acme,100.00
2,Globex,20.50`}
    >
      <code>unzip -p</code> streams the entry to stdout — no temp file, no cleanup.
    </ShellRecipe>
  </section>

  <CodeBlock title="sales.excsv.pack.zip" description="A pack archive holding the same orders table, one column per entry.">
{`sales.excsv.pack.zip
├── _manifest.excsv
└── orders/
    ├── _header.excsv
    ├── 00-id.col
    ├── 01-customer.col
    └── 02-amount.col`}
  </CodeBlock>

  <section class="cb-section">
    <h2>Pack — read one column, not the whole file</h2>
    <p class="section-note">
      A <code>.excsv.pack.zip</code> stores each column as its own <code>.col</code> entry inside a per-table folder.
      That means you can read a single column out of a wide table without decompressing the rest of it.
    </p>

    <ShellRecipe
      title="List tables and columns without extracting anything"
      command={`zipinfo -1 sales.excsv.pack.zip`}
      output={`_manifest.excsv
orders/_header.excsv
orders/00-id.col
orders/01-customer.col
orders/02-amount.col`}
    />

    <ShellRecipe
      title="Read one column, untouched by the rest"
      command={`unzip -p sales.excsv.pack.zip orders/02-amount.col`}
      output={`100.00
20.50`}
    >
      Only that one <code>.col</code> entry gets inflated — the other columns, and other tables in the archive, are never touched. See <a href="/variants/#pack">Pack</a>.
    </ShellRecipe>
  </section>

  <section class="cb-outro">
    <h2>Where this stops working</h2>
    <p>
      These commands assume well-formed input and don't validate, handle quoted delimiters inside
      fields, follow multi-line <code>#$ddl</code> continuations, or resolve computed
      (<code>formula=</code>) columns. That's the line between a cookbook and a parser — cross it
      with <a href="/tools/">excsv-golang</a> or <a href="/tools/">excsv-python</a>. Every recipe
      above was run against the project's <a href="https://github.com/boligolov/excsv/tree/master/fixtures" target="_blank" rel="noopener">fixture corpus</a>; if you find one that breaks on a real file, <a href="https://github.com/boligolov/excsv/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

